CPython's numeric, tuple and exception tests (tests/upstream_*.py)
------------------------------------------------------------------

These differential tests and rejection fixtures adapt test methods of CPython 3.13.16's
Lib/test/ at commit cbc944f4bc59639a444dd971c737788ba2283a91:

- tests/upstream_int_*.py and tests/errors/upstream_int_*.py: Lib/test/test_int.py and
  Lib/test/test_long.py (with the underscore literals of Lib/test/support/numbers.py)
- tests/upstream_float_*.py: Lib/test/test_float.py (with Lib/test/mathdata/
  formatfloat_testcases.txt and floating_points.txt, and Lib/test/support/numbers.py's
  underscore literals)
- tests/upstream_math_*.py (but upstream_math_fmod.py, under CPython's math tests above) and
  tests/errors/upstream_math_prod_*.py: Lib/test/test_math.py (with
  Lib/test/mathdata/math_testcases.txt, the real cases of cmath_testcases.txt, and the doctest
  ieee754.txt)
- tests/upstream_bool.py and tests/errors/upstream_bool_*.py: Lib/test/test_bool.py
- tests/upstream_tuple_seq.py and tests/errors/upstream_tuple_*.py: Lib/test/test_tuple.py and
  the methods of Lib/test/seq_tests.py that its TupleTest runs
- tests/upstream_exceptions_*.py and tests/errors/upstream_exceptions_*.py:
  Lib/test/test_exceptions.py
- tests/upstream_baseexception.py and tests/errors/upstream_baseexception_*.py:
  Lib/test/test_baseexception.py (with Lib/test/exception_hierarchy.txt)

They are used under the Python Software Foundation License Version 2 reproduced above;
tests/upstream/CPYTHON-LICENSE.txt retains CPython's full license. Each file's header names the
upstream test methods it adapts and lists, with the reason, those it leaves out.

Summary of changes: remove unittest and test.support scaffolding (each test method becomes a
function, assertEqual a printed value and an assert, assertRaises a try statement, and each
doctest example a printed value and an assert), embed the data files the tests read, add type
annotations where Pystachy's subset needs them, write out the loops over builtin exception
classes and over values of several types, replace float.fromhex(), math.isclose() and
itertools.product() with small equivalents in the files that use them, draw random data from a
fixed linear congruential sequence, run the loops over large integers only as far as their
values fit in 64 bits, write testHypotAccuracy's expected values (upstream computes them with
decimal) as the floats CPython 3.13.16 computes, turn into expected compile-time rejections the
errors that Pystachy reports at compile time with CPython's message (among them the syntax
errors that upstream checks with compile()), and leave out the cases that use what the subset
does not support. Differential output is recorded with CPython 3.13.16.

